# Retires the old host `groundworkllc.netlify.app`.
#
# This directory is not part of the site. It is deployed to the Netlify site
# `groundworkllc` as a manual deploy, in place of a build, so that every request
# to the old host 301s to the same path on the new domain. The retired Groundwork
# pages stop being served; inbound links and bookmarks keep resolving.
#
#   npx netlify-cli deploy --prod --dir docs/netlify-retire \
#     --site 38200c77-154d-4ee6-a062-578b904cec1a
#
# One catch-all on purpose. Netlify substitutes a placeholder only when it is a
# whole path segment, so a `/ :page.html` style rule is emitted literally and
# 404s - tried on 2026-11-22 and reverted the same hour. The cost of the catch-all
# is one extra hop for `.html` links: the old host 301s to the same path, and
# Pages 308s that to the extensionless URL. The alternative is enumerating forty
# paths, which is worse to maintain than a second redirect no visitor notices.
#
# `301!` is forced, so the rule applies to paths that could not otherwise be
# matched. Nothing exists in this directory but this file, so that is every path,
# including `/`. The same catch-all lives in `netlify.toml` for the day a
# git-triggered build of `dist/` deploys instead; both routes converge on the same
# redirect, and either one retires the old brand.

/*  https://waxwinglandscaping.com/:splat  301!
